Commands used:
nano - Terminal text editor.
python3 -m http.server - Turns the computer into a quick and easy web server.
ps - Provides a list of the running processes and some additional info like how much usage time of the CPU the program is using.
ps aux - Provides a list of processes run by other users and those that don't run from a session.
kill - Shuts down the process with the provided PID. For example kill 1337 .
systemctl - Allows the user to start, stop, enable, disable and status a process. For example: systemctl stop myservice .

notes:
Exiting nano - Exit nano using (Ctrl + X).
VIM - More advanced text editor. Keyboard shortcuts, syntax highlighting, works on all terminals where nano may not be installed.
wget - Allows the user to download files from the web via http, as if the user was accessing the file in his browser. Just provide the address and that's it.
SCP (Secure copy) - Just a means of securely copying files. Unlike the regular cp command, this allows the user to transfer files between two cvomputers using the SSH to provide both authentication and encryption.
SCP examples - scp important.txt ubuntu@192.168.1.30:/home/ubuntu/transferred.txt (SENDS A FILE TO A REMOTE COMPUTER). - scp ubuntu@192.168.1.30:/home/ubuntu/documents.txt notes.txt (TAKES A FILE FROM A REMOTE COMPUTER).
HTTPServer - Python3 provided module, which allows the user to turn the computer into a web server that you can use to serve your own files, where they can be downloaded by another computing using commands like curl and wget.
PID - Processes have a PID like the 60th process will have a PID of 60.
kill command variations - SIGTERM: Kills the process, but allows it to do some cleanup tasks beforehand. SIGKILL: Kills the process and doesn't do any clean up after. SIGSTOP: Stop/Suspend a process.
Pausing a script - (Ctrl + Z), instead of relying on & operator.
Backgrounding a process - & operator lets the command run in the background and you can pause it with (Ctrl + Z).
Foregrounding a process - fg command to bring back the running command into focus
Systemd - Usually the first process that starts when booting computer so PID 1. Used to provide a way of managing a user's processes and sits in between the operating system and the user.

cron and crontabs - Crontab is one of the processes that is started during boot. Crontab is recognised by the cron process to execute each line step-by-step. Crontabs require 6 values: MIN, HOUR, DOM, MON, DOW and CMD.
MIN: What minute to execute at. HOUR: what hour to execute at. DOM: What day of the month to execute at. MON: What month of the year to execute at. DOW: What day of the week to execute at. CMD: The acutal command that is executed
cron example: 0 */12 * * * cp -R /home/cmnatic/Documents /var/backups/

apt (Advanced package tool) - Allows the user to install, remove, update and manage software packages.
log monitoring - /var/log/ .

